write a personalised 1-week gym plan for a client, based on their onboarding details below. Write it the way a real coach would send it — clear and structured where it needs to be (exercises, sets, reps), but with a personal, conversational voice around it, not like an auto-generated template.

CLIENT DETAILS:
[paste client details here]

HOW TO WRITE IT:

1. Start with a short personal note (2-4 sentences) addressing the client by name — reference their goal and something specific from their answers (schedule, injury history, experience level). Keep it casual, like a coach checking in.

2. Opening mesocycle (month 1, week 1): pick a split that fits THIS client's goal, schedule, injuries, training history, and equipment. Proven templates are fine (full body, upper/lower, PPL) when they match the days and session duration. Personalise exercise selection; do not invent an unsafe novelty split just to be unique, and do not default to a random chest/back/arms bro-split. Name each day as "Day N (Weekday): Focus" where Day 1 is Monday through Day 7 Sunday (e.g. "Day 1 (Monday): Lower Power", "Day 2 (Tuesday): Upper Hypertrophy"). Never use bare "Day N" alone or weekday-only headers — the daily tracker needs the weekday in parentheses. Use BASE (lowest) volume for week 1: 2 to 3 working sets per exercise, about 5 to 7 working exercises per session, and leave 2 to 3 reps in reserve on compounds. Never prescribe 5 or more working sets. Do not write calorie targets in the workout; food lives in the diet plan. Never use hyphen, en dash, or em dash characters in client-facing text.

3. Reps by experience: beginners 8 to 15 on compounds. Intermediate 6 to 12. Advanced or strength-focused: 3 to 6 on main compounds only (squat, deadlift, bench, overhead press, rows), 8 to 15 on accessories. Isolation/machine work: 8 to 15 reps. Default 2 to 3 working sets. Advanced clients may use 4 working sets on ONE main compound only. Warm-up sets do not count as working sets.

4. Include 1 core finisher per training day (2 max on a dedicated core day). Do not add three or four extra ab circuits on every session — that inflates junk volume.

5. Adjust exercises for any limitations the client mentioned (e.g. no pull-ups → lat pulldown, can't squat → leg press/hack squat, no pushups → machine press).

6. Open the plan with a short warmup routine (around 10 minutes) — brisk walk/light cardio, joint mobility, a few bodyweight movements — written as a simple flowing list rather than a rigid numbered table. Mention doing 2 lighter warm-up sets before the first heavy compound lift, phrased naturally (e.g. "before you get into your main lifts, run through a couple of lighter warm-up sets").

7. For each training day, list exercises with sets/reps clearly using "Exercise: N sets x M reps" or "N sets x M to K reps" (plain letter x). Add a short coaching note here and there — a cue, a tip on tempo, what to focus on, written like a coach's aside rather than a formal instruction block.
   Name lifts in ordinary coach English with the tool when it matters (Barbell Bench Press, Dumbbell Romanian Deadlift, Resistance Band Row). One movement per line. No nicknames, no two lifts in one name. Do not copy a vendor catalog if that would break equipment or injury constraints.
   CRITICAL: Every training day must list every exercise with sets x reps in full under that day. NEVER write "same as Day 1", "repeat Monday", "follow Thursday's workout", "as above", or any cross-day pointer. If two days use the same session, rewrite the complete exercise list under both day headers. The daily tracker cannot resolve those references.
   Keep each day self-contained. If you add Post-Workout stretches, put them under that same day only. Never put the next day's lifts under Post-Workout / Recovery / Stretching of the previous day. Do not use a lone "Recovery" or "Stretching" line between days.

8. You MAY weave a one-line daily step target into the week (training days a bit higher, rest days a bit lower) using Metabolic Flux Bias (BUILD-UP / HIGH FLUX raise steps above their current habit; STEADY stays modest). Do not add a Cardio, Conditioning, or Supplements section header. Full cardio is generated separately.

9. Label all 7 calendar days. Training days per week is a hard cap — program exactly that many lifting sessions, not more. Remaining days are rest or active recovery. Do not require both a dedicated recovery day AND a rest day if that would steal a training day or overflow 7 days. If they train 6 days, one rest. If 7, no extra rest day. When flux bias is BUILD-UP or HIGH FLUX, fill allowed session duration with quality work (2 to 3 working sets, 5 to 7 exercises), not extra junk sets.

10. Mention training intensity guidance in a natural paragraph or two rather than a formal rulebook — leave a rep or two in reserve on compounds, push isolation work closer to failure, and only allow a couple of controlled "cheat reps" on the last set of isolation moves after strict failure (never on squats, deadlifts, RDLs, lunges, or any barbell compound). State this clearly but conversationally.

11. End the week with a short stretching/cooldown routine covering the major muscle groups trained, with rough hold times (30 to 60 seconds each side), written casually. Place this shared cooldown AFTER all Day N blocks (not between days).

12. Add a short sleep note — duration target (7.5 to 9 hours) and a few practical tips suited to an Indian lifestyle (chai cutoff, screens, meal timing). Friendly suggestions, not a clinical list, and not a Cardio or Supplements section.

13. Close with a short, encouraging note for the week — no formal "Weekly Focus" bullet section, no "TRAINING INTENSITY RULES" headers, no after-week guidance.

14. Cover all 7 days as "Day N (Weekday)" with Day 1 = Monday through Day 7 = Sunday. Never skip a required training day. Balance upper and lower body across the week — don't overload either.

15. Don't mention the number of week it is, just skip mentioning it

16. **Hard constraints:** Obey the Hard Constraints section — preferred exercises, exercises to avoid, injuries, training days, and gym equipment only. If anything else in this prompt conflicts with Hard Constraints, obey Hard Constraints.

TONE: Write like an experienced coach messaging a client they train personally — direct and practical for the workout details (sets/reps/exercises can stay in clean list form, that's expected), but wrapped in natural, encouraging language for the intros, transitions, cues, and tips. Avoid robotic section headers like "DAILY STEP TARGET" or "TRAINING INTENSITY RULES" — fold that information into the flow of the plan instead.
